    ],
    "reading_order": 8,
    "text": "2 ,\n2,\n3,\n15,\n,\n0.10110111011110 . . .\n3\n\n, etc.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 6,
    "block_id": "jemh101_p006_b09",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      320.16,
      420.04,
      345.56
    ],
    "reading_order": 9,
    "text": "Before we prove that \n2 is irrational, we need the following theorem, whose\nproof is based on the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 6,
    "block_id": "jemh101_p006_b10",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      357.99,
      419.96,
      383.6
    ],
    "reading_order": 10,
    "text": "Theorem 1.2 : Let p be a prime number. If p divides a2, then p divides a, where\na is a positive integer.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 6,
