    "text": "1p2\n2 . . . p2\nn.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 6,
    "block_id": "jemh101_p006_b15",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      446.67,
      420.03,
      503.83
    ],
    "reading_order": 15,
    "text": "Now, we are given that p divides a2. Therefore, from the Fundamental Theorem of\nArithmetic, it follows that p is one of the prime factors of a2. However, using the\nuniqueness part of the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic, we realise that the only\nprime factors of a2 are p1, p2, . . ., pn. So p is one of p1, p2, . . ., pn.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH101",
    "page": 6,
    "block_id": "jemh101_p006_b16",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      508.8,
      227.99,
      522.43
    ],
    "reading_order": 16,
    "text": "Now, since a = p1 p2 . . . pn, p divides a.",
    "latex": null,
